        definition:
          'Primary ionisation alone cannot sustain a discharge, because the avalanche ends when its electrons reach the anode. A self-sustaining discharge needs the avalanche to regenerate its own starting electrons, and it does so by secondary processes at the cathode: positive ions drifting back bombard the cathode and liberate electrons, and photons from excited molecules do the same. The second Townsend coefficient, gamma, is the number of secondary electrons released per positive ion arriving. Including it modifies the current growth equation with a denominator that can approach zero.',
        takeaway: 'Gamma closes the loop by regenerating starting electrons at the cathode; the denominator approaching zero is breakdown.',
