          'An electronegative gas has molecules that readily capture free electrons to form negative ions, removing those electrons from the avalanche and converting them into heavy, slow carriers that cannot ionise further. The attachment coefficient measures this capture rate per centimetre, and the effective ionisation coefficient is the primary ionisation coefficient less the attachment coefficient. Breakdown requires the effective coefficient to be positive, so an electronegative gas needs a substantially higher field before it breaks down at all. Sulphur hexafluoride is the practical example, with roughly two and a half times the strength of air.',
        takeaway: 'Attachment eats electrons out of the avalanche, so effective alpha is alpha minus eta — and SF6 exploits it.',
