        definition:
          'A single transformer for very high alternating voltage becomes impractical because its insulation to earth must withstand the full output. Cascading solves this by using several transformers whose tanks sit at progressively higher potential, each contributing its own secondary voltage to the stack. Each unit needs insulation only for its own stage voltage relative to its tank, though its supporting insulators must withstand the accumulated potential. The penalty is that the lowest transformer must carry the power for the entire cascade, so the units are rated unequally and the bottom one is by far the largest.',
