        topic: 'The electrostatic voltmeter',
        slides: [26, 27, 28],
        terms: ['electrostatic voltmeter', 'attractive force', 'square law', 'no current', 'absolute instrument'],
        definition:
          'An electrostatic voltmeter measures voltage by the mechanical force of attraction between two charged electrodes, one fixed and one movable against a spring or suspension. Because the force depends on the square of the voltage, the instrument responds to the root mean square value regardless of waveform, which is a genuine advantage over methods that weight harmonics. It draws no steady current at all, so it does not load the source, and since the force can be computed from the geometry it can serve as an absolute instrument requiring no calibration against another.',
        takeaway: 'Force between plates goes as voltage squared, so it reads true root mean square with no current drawn.',
        visual: 'electrostatic-voltmeter',
