        definition:
          'Four methods cover the range of high current measurement. A resistive shunt of very low value develops a measurable voltage proportional to current, and is used for impulse currents where its low inductance construction matters more than its dissipation. A Rogowski coil is an air-cored toroid whose output is proportional to the rate of change of current and must be integrated, giving isolation and no saturation. A Hall generator responds to the magnetic field and therefore reads direct current, which neither of the others can. Magnetic links are small pieces of magnetic material whose remanent magnetisation records the peak current passed, used for lightning surveys where no instrument can be stationed.',
        takeaway: 'Shunt for impulses, Rogowski for isolation, Hall for direct current, magnetic link for unattended lightning records.',
