        definition:
          'A perfect dielectric draws a current leading the voltage by exactly ninety degrees and dissipates no power. A real dielectric draws a current leading by slightly less, and the shortfall from ninety degrees is the loss angle, conventionally written delta. Its tangent, the loss tangent or dissipation factor, is the ratio of the resistive to the capacitive component of the current and is the standard measure of dielectric quality. Because it is a ratio it is independent of specimen size, which makes it directly comparable between different pieces of apparatus and across time.',
        takeaway: 'The shortfall from a perfect ninety degrees is the loss angle, and its tangent is a size-independent quality measure.',
